# Therapy Prompt Lessons

## 2026-10-28 — Meet untellable shame without alarm, grade the telling, and name the tool's limits

Owner-requested general rule (request of 26 Oct 2026; no client information is recorded). Strong shame often guards something the person believes they can't tell anyone, and secrecy can add to the self-rejection, though relief never depends on telling anyone. InnerSignal reads such material as ordinary therapeutic material, names what kind it is (feeling, fantasy, dream, uncertain memory, accidental exposure, chosen act), keeps it apart from the person's worth and from intent, and never answers with alarm, moralizing or a refusal script. It helps the person choose whom to tell and how much, with advice to give them that starts general and watches how a smaller truth is received, without pressing and without hiding current danger. Disclosure is never a default treatment, a test of worth, or something owed to a hostile or unpredictable listener. Early in the work, after the person has been welcomed, it says once that some topics may be beyond what it can help with; when one is, it names the limit as the tool's and not a verdict on the person, and points to a human who can help. It offers a therapist experienced with early sexual trauma when the person feels or suspects early sexual harm, without asserting that abuse happened. When the person says they saw illegal material by accident, it says that this is not seeking it and gives advice to pass on: not to save or share the material, that reporting the material itself is the person's own choice, and to ask a lawyer about any legal worry; InnerSignal reports no one and does not assess legal exposure. It routes material for outside review only on evidence of current risk to someone, never for taboo content, dreams, fantasies or a past accidental exposure. Amendment: AMEND.CROSS.UNTELLABLE_SHAME_AND_DISCLOSURE_20261028. Operational prompts (untellable-shame-v1), the mirrored section of the phenomenology reference and graph nodes carry the duties; the reader-facing guide is not changed by this lesson. Development source is not proof of installed or reliably executed therapy behavior.

## 2026-10-26 — Preserve feeling/action distinctions, referral fit, and pre-peak crisis evidence

Owner-approved general corrections: unchosen feelings are not misconduct; deliberate action remains accountable to consent, protection and trusted boundaries. Avoid speculative explanations for taboo thoughts or promised changes through therapy. Before sensitive referrals, verify the client's actual need, prior professional care and privacy limits. When records already establish that strategies fail in overwhelming crises, compare when those same strategies work during earlier escalation instead of re-asking the failed peak question. Amendments: AMEND.CROSS.FEELING_AGENCY_AND_REFERRAL_FIT_20261026 and AMEND.CROSS.EARLY_REGULATION_CONDITION_20261026. Operational prompts, graph, served references and public-guide queue PGQ-043 carry the duties. No client information belongs in this repository. Development source is not proof of installed or reliably executed therapy behavior.

## 2026-10-28 — Keep what a client saw, read, suspects and believes apart, at the size they said it

**Development candidate, not deployed.** Owner-requested rule from a de-identified lesson document (27 October): when a client's worldview includes disputed institutional, historical or theological claims, keep four kinds of statement apart and attributed: what the client took part in or witnessed, what they studied later, their own hypotheses connecting sources or organizations, and their broader claims about institutions, motives or power. A first-person account is a report to respect, not a guarantee of every remembered detail; a wider inference does not become fact by repetition. Keep each claim's subject and strength exactly as given: an allegation about a named subset, a purported elite or specific conduct is never restated as a claim about a whole ethnic, religious or national community, and topical resemblance alone is not proof of character, prejudice, intent or danger. Listening respectfully is not agreeing that an unverified network exists, so do not affirm it to keep rapport. Unless the belief changes a therapeutic or safety decision or the client explicitly asks for an evidence review, stay on their goal (belonging, connection, safety, coping, relationships, reparenting); a disputed view is not by itself a symptom, and a request for historical or theological research is its own task, examined honestly. When the belief affects how they treat others, restricts their agency, prompts an unsafe decision or bears on threats or coercion, engage the actual behavior and evidence proportionately and let safety override parking the topic. Keep honest criticism of wrongdoing they genuinely witnessed or suffered. Two audit codes carry this, `CLAIM_SCOPE_SUBSTITUTION` and `UNREQUESTED_CLAIM_ADJUDICATION`. Both are recorded and sent to repair without withholding the fallback question for now, because how often a real model flags a careful reply by mistake is not yet measured; making the scope code blocking is one entry in `ROLE_BELIEF_BLOCKING_CODES` once a model run shows it is reliable.

Authority and evidence: the owner's request of 27 October 2026; `role-belief-integrity-v3` in `src/prompts/role-belief-integrity.mjs` and the served `ROLE-BELIEF-INTEGRITY.md`. The same document's lessons on known versus new, reparenting focus and unsent replies were already served (`LONGITUDINAL-SYNTHESIS.md`, `FOCUS-DISCIPLINE.md`, the skill's supervisor-edit section and `RESPONSE-FIDELITY.md`); delivery states are being made mechanical in a separate change. `tests/worldview-evidence-scope.test.mjs` with `corpus/worldview-evidence-scope-cases.json` (invented people, groups and country) checks delivery to every response, review and repair role and the scripted runtime's handling of the codes, not model adherence. No client information is in this repository.

## 2026-10-25 — Preserve actual delivery, assemble the priority focus once, and learn from existing coping

**Development candidate, not live.** Owner-approved general response changes follow a failure to keep the prioritized therapeutic target in one place, a false reference to unsent advice, premature generic cessation guidance despite recorded history, too indirect a question about a valued subjective state, and silent narrowing of an inclusive group in translation. Requirements: know what reached the recipient; keep the current focus and its first-priority question in a contiguous passage; use the journal and interventions before introducing generalized risk/withdrawal assumptions; find the person's most important experienced function and already-tested non-harmful methods; distinguish partial body relaxation from whole-person calm; test relapse-access barriers against real availability and feasibility; translate with unchanged inclusion and attribution. Maintain safety overrides, capacity, and consent. The already-sent client message is not revised or resent.

Generalized source: AMEND.CROSS.REPLY_FOCUS_AND_DELIVERY_20261025. Operational surfaces: shared focus and response rules, MCP-served therapy references, existing graph nodes, pending public-guide queue. Technical tests check source/protocol parity and structural fixtures; live model adherence remains unverified. No private case content or client identifiers are stored in Git.

## 2026-10-28 — Recover what each person said and did before interpreting a reported rupture

**Development candidate, not deployed.** Owner-requested general correction (private specifics excluded). An account that runs from wanting acceptance, to a disagreement or confrontation, to rejection often arrives without its middle, and a reply that explains it before the middle is known can credit the wrong person, treat wanting love as proof that a confrontation was loving, treat another person's discomfort as proof of coercion or of rejecting the person's beliefs, or reach for an old developmental pattern the sequence would not support. Requirements: before interpreting, recover what was first said and done, what each person said and did (the client's own words and actions as well as the other person's), the timing and any boundaries stated, and how the client read the result; when that is missing, ask one specific, non-accusatory question whose plausible answers would change the next step (protective action, accountability or communication work, or developmental integration), and do not repeat a question already answered. Genuine exclusion, a legitimate boundary in response to confrontational behavior, mixed escalation and an unknown chronology all stay possible until the sequence says which fits; anger is not hatred or current harmful intent; where threats or intimidation are actually supported, the existing safety and accountability distinctions stand and the threatened party is not held equally responsible; childhood imagery is never forced onto the account.

**Authority and propagation:** `AMEND.CROSS.RELATIONAL_SEQUENCE_BOTH_SIDES_20261028` (owner-approved 2026-10-28), the existing `ROUTE.RELATIONAL_REALITY_CHECK` node (its first recommendation and its own next question now cover both sides), one bullet in the `focus-discipline-v1` rules (shared prompts and the served `FOCUS-DISCIPLINE.md`) and the `MISSING_MIDDLE_INTERPRETATION` audit failure in `RESPONSE-FIDELITY.md`; no new evaluator. `tests/relational-sequence.test.mjs` and the synthetic scenes in `corpus/relational-sequence-regression-cases.json` (invented people) check that the rule, the audit code, the amendment and the graph node agree and that each scene has a differentiated pass criterion; they do not show that any model follows the rule. The public-guide sentence is the owner's question 46, part 2, and is applied separately; `authoring/PENDING-PUBLIC-GUIDE-CHANGES.md` is untouched. No private case content or client identifiers are stored in Git.

## 2026-10-31 — Treat the performed self as a survival skill, not a fake part, and keep care from turning permissive or forced

**Development candidate, not deployed.** Owner-approved alignment of the app's rules with two passages of AI guide r8 that its graph did not yet carry; the wording is the owner's, with no new therapy claim and no new owner amendment (private specifics excluded). First, where childhood was organized around avoiding pain and preserving attachment, attention went into reading danger, meeting other people's expectations and keeping the bond, and the performed self got better at surviving while the less-practiced self stayed blurry. Recovery includes more than uncovering a fully formed "true self" hidden underneath: some of it is developmental pl/ork (play + work) done late, creating enough safety for preference, curiosity and experimentation to grow. The performed self is never set up as a "fake" part to erase; the aim is a more workable internal relationship, not a new division between an acceptable self and another self that must disappear. Second, without direction, care for oneself, reparenting included, can become permissive (attention without development, with candy, screens and escape from anything difficult masquerading as self-love); the opposite trap is forced adulthood (perfect discipline, no visible needs, very little joy or closeness), where the next adult act may be asking for help, expressing a preference or letting a safe person see something imperfect, not tightening control again. The two are always named together, and the warning also appears early, beside the small-action guidance, so it does not wait for the late Guide node; it says "escape from anything difficult" so that ending an unsafe exchange or asking for help is still protection.

**Authority and propagation:** AI guide r8 sections `IC.IDENTITY_PLAY` and `IC.GUIDE_LATER` (no new amendment); the existing nodes `IC.IDENTITY_FORMATION` (one recommendation, one avoid line), `IC.GUIDE_LATER` (one avoid line) and `IC.PROTECTOR_ACTION` (one avoid line after its "action so large" line, with `IC.GUIDE_LATER` added to its sources); two adjacent bullets in the response-fidelity rules (shared prompts and the served `RESPONSE-FIDELITY.md`, byte for byte); no new plugin file, evaluator, audit code, node, edge or amendment. `tests/performed-self-permissive-reparenting.test.mjs` and the synthetic scenes in `corpus/performed-self-permissive-regression-cases.json` (invented people) check that the wording stays inside the guide's words and that each line is bound, compiled, planned, served and mirrored; they do not show that any model follows the rules. `authoring/PENDING-PUBLIC-GUIDE-CHANGES.md` gains PGQ-050 (the owner's schema-therapy comparison, written at his request on 31 October 2026, for the humanized public guide only). No private case content or client identifiers are stored in Git.

## 2026-10-31 — After a painful disagreement, compare what was intended, what was expected and how it is judged now

**Development candidate, not deployed.** Owner-approved (question 51, option A, answered 31 October 2026, 12:43 UTC) extension of the missing-middle lesson of 28 October (private specifics excluded). Once the sequence of what each person said and did is known well enough, the reply reviews the completed encounter before choosing a next step: what the client was trying to achieve by communicating, the reaction they realistically anticipated at the time (including surprise or a foreseeable tradeoff), and how they now judge both their manner and what followed, then compares the three. That is not the same as defining a realistic outcome for a future interaction. Nothing is assumed about regret, fault, satisfaction, a wish to reconcile or a fixed character: a client can stand by a position, regret the wording and grieve the loss at once. The comparison chooses among communication repair, a boundary, a decision about relational fit and optional developmental work. Once the sequence is known, incident detail stops being requested unless it would change the next step or safety; when context already suggests what the client hoped for, the more useful questions are whether they expected the reaction and how they judge their choice now, and this is never a requirement to ask all three every time. Where threats or intimidation are actually supported, the existing safety and accountability distinctions stay, and the threatening and the threatened party are not equally responsible. Closely related questions that serve one decision may form one coherent inquiry, weighing the value of the answers against the load on the person, with one primary next move and low-value subsidiary questions deferred.

**Authority and propagation:** `AMEND.CROSS.RELATIONAL_INTENTION_EXPECTATION_APPRAISAL_20261031` (owner-approved 2026-10-31; amendments `owner-amendments-2026-10-31-r21`), bound to the existing `ROUTE.RELATIONAL_REALITY_CHECK` (one recommendation and one avoid line; no new node, edge or question); one bullet in the `focus-discipline-v1` rules and one question-selection bullet in the response-fidelity rules, each mirrored byte for byte in the served references; no new evaluator, audit code or plugin file. AI guide r9 adds the owner's two sentences directly after the r8 sentence on going back over what each person said and did, and `authoring/PENDING-PUBLIC-GUIDE-CHANGES.md` gains PGQ-051 for the humanized guide. `tests/relational-intention-expectation.test.mjs`, the synthetic scenes in `corpus/relational-intention-expectation-regression-cases.json` (seven, invented people: an unexpected silence with regret about the wording, a foreseen and accepted distance, doubt about the belief itself, mixed feelings, a foreseen hostile reaction, a supported threat and a hope that is already clear, each with its own next step and with contrast pairs) and graph cases G142 to G144 check the wording, the binding, the compile, the plan and the mirrors; they do not show that any model follows the rules. One limit: while the relational check is pending, the planner still gives the route's own sequence question and the response contract replaces a reply's own final question with it, and the case variables do not say whether the sequence is already known, so in the app the appraisal reaches a reply's body through the rules and the route's recommendations, not through a question of its own; a reply written from the served protocol references is not subject to that contract. No private case content or client identifiers are stored in Git.

## 2026-10-31 — Reject the insult in a critical inner voice without presuming it is a protector, and offer drawing for anger that has no words

**Development candidate, not deployed.** Owner-approved refinements (issue 215, 31 October 2026; private specifics excluded; the two books the issue compares are inspiration only and none of their prose is used). A critical inner voice may be replaying something learned from a parent, trying to protect the person from rejection or failure, pointing to a real problem in a cruel way, or doing several of these at once. The reply therefore helps the person reject the insult, keep whatever is useful and any real responsibility, protect themselves without first settling which it is, and ask what the voice is afraid of or trying to accomplish only if that helps; understanding where the criticism comes from is not agreeing with it or letting it keep attacking, and a critic is neither assumed to be a protector nor made an enemy. The guide's list of proactive protectors names a self-critical strategy that is trying to prevent a feared outcome instead of listing the inner critic unconditionally. One optional nonverbal practice is added for anger that is hard to put into words, drawing or scribbling it, with nothing required to be recognizable, no claim that it reveals a hidden memory or brings a release, and no new session stage. Not imported: an "Inner Brat" part, a catharsis theory, a non-dominant-hand or right-hemisphere mechanism, a claim that repressed emotion causes disease, or automatic authority for a "Spiritual Child".

